   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "What a person is like",
     "texto": [
      "To describe character we use **ser** + adjective: Marta **es sincera**, Elena **es habladora**.\n\nMany adjectives have an opposite made with a prefix:\n\n- **paciente → impaciente** (before p, in- becomes im-)\n- **seguro → inseguro**\n- **educado → maleducado**\n\nThere are also expressions with **carácter**: **tener mucho carácter** (to be strong and determined) and **tener mal carácter** (to get angry easily). **Tener sentido del humor** means knowing how to laugh.\n\nWith **parecerse a** we talk about inherited traits: Elena **se parece a** su padre. And with **portarse bien / mal**, about how a person behaves."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Opposites, ser and estar",
     "texto": [
      "Opposites are formed in several ways:\n\n- **in- / im-**: seguro → **in**seguro · paciente → **im**paciente\n- **des-**: agradable → **des**agradable\n- **mal-**: educado → **mal**educado\n\nWith **ser** we say what a person is always like: Marta **es** paciente. With **estar**, how they feel right now: today she **está** impaciente.\n\nThe adjective agrees with the person: Marta es introvertid**a** · Pablo es introvertid**o**."
     ],
